Revision history for Tie::Cache::Autoupdater

0.10
        First version, released on an unsuspecting world.
0.20
        Added new attribute - "clone". Delete unnecessary warn message when key doesn't exist
0.21
        Bugfix. Check data type before returned dclone reference
0.22
        Bugfix. FIRSTKEY and NEXTKEY returned cached values instead of key
        names, so "keys %cache" listed values and "each %cache" could not be
        used at all. Iterating an empty cache also created an entry under an
        empty key. They now return key names, as the tie interface requires.

        Rewrote the documentation and the README, and documented behaviour
        that was previously undescribed: how a source's return value is
        normalised, what happens when a source dies, what "clone" does and
        does not copy, and how the standard hash operations behave.

        Test suite extended to cover the whole module (statements, branches,
        conditions and subroutines). Verified on perl 5.26 - 5.42.

        Added a LICENSE file with the full license text and fixed the license
        declared in Makefile.PL: it said Artistic 2.0, while the documentation
        has always said "the same terms as Perl itself" (Artistic 1.0 or GPL).

        Removed the empty TODO file from the distribution.
0.23
        Bugfix. On perl older than 5.18, reading a key registered without a
        source emitted an extra "Use of uninitialized value in subroutine
        entry" warning before the module's own "Check source subroutine"
        warning. Only the module's warning is emitted now, on every perl.

        Bugfix. A failing source could be taken for one returning nothing,
        and its "result" cached as undef for the whole timeout, with no
        warning: on perl older than 5.14 when a destructor running eval
        cleared $@ as the source died (DBI handles, guard objects), and on
        any perl when the source died with a false exception object. Failure
        is now detected from eval's return value; an empty error is reported
        as "unknown error".

        Timeouts are now measured on the monotonic clock where the system
        has one (via Time::HiRes), so setting the system clock back - by hand
        or by NTP - no longer keeps a stale value cached until the clock
        catches up, and setting it forward no longer expires values early.
        Without a monotonic clock Time::HiRes::time or the built-in time is
        used, as before.

        Declared the minimum perl version, 5.8, in Makefile.PL and in the
        documentation. The GitHub CI matrix now covers perl 5.8.9 through 5.42.
